## Problem

The typed browser tools can snapshot and mutate page content, but a page-owned JavaScript dialog (`alert`, `confirm`, `prompt`, or `beforeunload`) blocks the tab and currently has no explicit browser-tool contract. Agents may see subsequent CDP commands stall or fail without a safe way to identify and resolve the exact dialog.

This is distinct from browser-chrome permission prompts, extension UI, operating-system dialogs, and native file pickers. Those surfaces must remain native or return a structured refusal.

## Proposed contract

Add an exact-tab dialog state and action surface, either through `get_browser_state` plus a typed mutation or a dedicated browser-dialog tool. It must:

- bind to the caller session, exact `target_id`, `tab_id`, connection generation, frame, and current dialog generation;
- report only bounded dialog kind and presence by default, without placing dialog text in telemetry;
- require an explicit accept or dismiss choice;
- accept prompt text only for `prompt` dialogs and treat it as sensitive action input;
- refuse stale, ambiguous, browser-chrome, permission, download, and native-dialog targets;
- preserve inactive-tab and foreground posture;
- invalidate the dialog capability immediately after one action or navigation.

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] Cover `alert`, `confirm`, `prompt`, and `beforeunload` in the repo-owned browser fixture.
- [ ] Prove exact inactive-tab handling without selecting or raising the browser.
- [ ] Prove stale dialog refs and wrong-tab actions fail closed.
- [ ] Keep dialog text and prompt input out of routine telemetry and recordings unless an explicitly approved evidence mode says otherwise.
- [ ] Return a structured refusal for browser permission prompts, extension UI, and native dialogs.
- [ ] Add rows to the canonical standalone-browser matrix on macOS, Windows, Linux X11, and accepted Wayland lanes.

## Problem

Browser-native navigation and clicks can trigger downloads, but CUA Driver has no typed way to authorize the file side effect, choose a safe destination, observe progress, or prove completion. Falling back to a download shelf or operating-system save dialog loses exact tab identity and makes background behavior unreliable.

Uploads are tracked separately in #2240. This issue covers downloads initiated by an exactly bound page.

## Proposed contract

Add an approval-gated browser download operation and bounded status surface. It must:

- require the caller session and exact `target_id`/`tab_id`;
- require explicit approval before enabling or accepting a file write;
- confine output to a caller-approved destination root and reject traversal, links, device paths, and ambiguous filenames;
- correlate the download event with the exact tab and connection generation;
- expose bounded state such as pending, in-progress, completed, canceled, or refused without placing URLs, filenames, paths, or response headers in routine telemetry;
- avoid selecting or raising an inactive browser tab;
- fail closed for browser-chrome downloads, extension downloads, multiple ambiguous downloads, and native save dialogs.

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] Add deterministic small-file, cancellation, duplicate-name, traversal, and stale-session fixture cases.
- [ ] Prove the completed file is confined to the approved root and matches an expected size/hash oracle.
- [ ] Prove no native save dialog, focus steal, cursor movement, or leaked input occurs.
- [ ] Define cleanup and partial-file behavior after cancellation, daemon restart, and reconnect.
- [ ] Keep download URLs, names, paths, cookies, headers, and file contents out of routine telemetry.
- [ ] Add canonical rows for macOS, Windows, Linux X11, and accepted Wayland browser lanes.

## Summary

Publish a versioned OpenClaw profile in the Cua skill pack that preserves the canonical Cua operating guidance while requiring provider-native MCP tools from the selected OpenClaw binding. The profile must not instruct the model to invoke the Cua CLI, start or update the daemon, or select a transport.

This is an upstream dependency for [OpenClaw RFC 0025](https://github.com/openclaw/rfcs/pull/45) and complements the generic ClawHub publishing work in #2264.

## Problem

The canonical Cua skill correctly teaches sessions, snapshot-before-and-after, AX/PX dispatch, foreground delivery, browser-page routing, desktop escalation, verification, recording, and platform behavior. Its default transport guidance shells out to `cua-driver`.

A managed OpenClaw run may use a remote desktop node and an app-owned private embedded transport. Direct CLI instructions could select the wrong machine, use another Cua session, launch a second daemon, or bypass OpenClaw's selected provider route and policy.

OpenClaw needs guidance matched to the exact accepted driver and live tool schemas without maintaining a fork of Cua's operating model.

## Proposed profile

Add a profile such as `openclaw-mcp` to the versioned Cua skill bundle.

It should preserve:

- explicit `start_session` and `end_session` guidance;
- snapshot before and after every action;
- AX background, PX background, exact browser-page, foreground-delivery, and explicit desktop-escalation behavior;
- `effect`, `verified`, `degraded`, and `escalation` interpretation;
- element-index, browser-ref, target, tab, and coordinate lifetimes;
- browser input-trust rules;
- recording and replay guidance where the host exposes them;
- applicable macOS, Windows, Linux, browser, recording, and embedding companion documents.

It should replace transport and bootstrap instructions with:

- use only the provider-native tools assembled for the selected OpenClaw binding;
- never invoke Cua through shell or CLI for this run;
- never start, stop, install, or update the daemon;
- never choose a socket, handle, machine, provider, or native session id;
- treat the OpenClaw execution binding as the transport owner;
- use the exact live tool names and schemas supplied for the selected provider generation.

## Driver manifest and bundle contract

Extend the machine-readable driver manifest or skill manifest with:

- profile id and schema version;
- driver version and accepted range;
- skill bundle digest and companion-resource digests;
- required launch, embedded-transport, authorization, browser, recording, and helper capabilities;
- the supported tool/schema profile used by compatibility checks.

The profile and companion files should be readable through a digest-verified provider-bundle resource reader. Reading them must not require arbitrary shell or node filesystem access.

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] The driver ships a versioned OpenClaw MCP-first profile and matching companion resources.
- [ ] The profile contains no instruction to run the Cua CLI, start another daemon, select a socket, install dependencies, or update Cua during a managed run.
- [ ] The profile preserves the canonical Cua ladder and snapshot/verification invariants.
- [ ] `bring_to_front` remains a standalone platform-specific tool where applicable; the normal foreground rung uses `delivery_mode:"foreground"` after evidence of background failure.
- [ ] The profile describes the exact browser-page route without using the ambiguous term `browser-typed`.
- [ ] The manifest binds the profile and companion resources to an exact digest and compatible driver version.
- [ ] A missing or mismatched profile fails compatibility instead of falling back to generic Computer Use guidance.
- [ ] Recorded live `tools/list` fixtures prove that every documented branch exists in the accepted schema.
- [ ] A representative task completes through MCP-only tools and follows snapshot, action, verification, and escalation guidance.
- [ ] Platform companion resources are filtered for the selected node without changing their digests silently.

## Non-goals

- Replacing Cua's canonical skill with an OpenClaw-owned fork.
- Giving the skill authority to approve tools, mint consent, alter permission mode, or unlock desktop scope.
- Publishing a second universal action schema across providers.

## Related work

- OpenClaw RFC: https://github.com/openclaw/rfcs/pull/45
- Generic ClawHub publishing: #2264
- Permission modes: #2381
- Canonical skill: `libs/cua-driver/rust/Skills/cua-driver/SKILL.md`

## What changed

- replaces the experimental PiP preview with a responsive, resizable Agent View on macOS, Windows, and Linux (X11/XWayland);
- presents exact native windows and Chrome tabs as a miniature desktop while preserving mixed target form factors;
- uses platform-native visual treatments: a glass hardware-like macOS shell and Dock, Windows desktop/taskbar styling, and a GNOME-like Linux frame/dash;
- removes synthetic target title bars so each card preserves the exact captured target pixels;
- groups cards by existing lifecycle sessions without adding claim/release, leases, target movement, target resizing, or target-close semantics;
- removes obsolete experimental PiP aliases and keeps `--agent-view`, `--agent-view-geometry`, `agent_view`, and `agent_view_geometry`;
- updates CLI/config copy to describe the three implemented native backends.

## Contract boundary

Agent View is presentation over existing lifecycle sessions. CLI/MCP action targets remain unchanged, and session grouping is presentation metadata rather than ownership. Browser sessions can continue to address multiple tabs independently.

Resizing Agent View changes only the composition; it never moves or resizes the underlying target windows.

