## Summary

`cua-driver mcp-config --client opencode` still printed the OpenCode V1 shape (`mcp.<name>` plus `enabled: true`). OpenCode 2 reads servers under `mcp.servers` and uses `disabled`, not `enabled`, so the generated snippet was silently ignored.

This changes the OpenCode preset to:

```jsonc
{
  "$schema": "https://opencode.ai/config.json",
  "mcp": {
    "servers": {
      "cua-driver": {
        "type": "local",
        "command": ["<binary>", "mcp"],
        "codemode": false,
        "disabled": false
      }
    }
  }
}
```

`codemode: false` keeps the tools as normal tool calls so screenshot content stays an image block. The snippet also mentions the native-Wayland opt-in (`CUA_DRIVER_RS_ENABLE_WAYLAND=1`).

## Validation

- New `mcp_config_skills_cli_test` case asserts `mcp.servers`, `codemode`, `disabled`, and the absence of `enabled`.
- `cargo test -p cua-driver --test mcp_config_skills_cli_test the_opencode_snippet` — passed.
- Manually verified the V2 server shape against OpenCode 2.2.5 (the source of the V2 docs).

### Primary area

Cua Driver

### Summary

On macOS, `launch_app` with `bundle_id: "com.apple.finder"` and an existing folder URL always fails after 5 s when the daemon runs with `serve --no-overlay`. The Finder folder handoff waits on the AppKit main queue, but in that mode nothing on the daemon's main thread serves the main queue.

### Reproduction

1. Start the daemon without the overlay: `cua-driver serve --socket <path> --no-overlay`. Accessibility and Screen Recording are granted.
2. Call `launch_app` with `bundle_id: "com.apple.finder"`, `urls: ["<an existing folder>"]`, `creates_new_application_instance: false`, and no extra arguments or environment.
3. The call fails after about 5 s. It fails on every call, and also after a restart of macOS.

It reproduces every time. With the overlay on (the default), the same call works.

### Expected behavior

Finder opens a window for the folder, as it does when the overlay is on.

### Actual behavior

`launch_app` fails with:

```
Launch failed: Timed out waiting for Finder folder handoff on the AppKit main queue: timed out waiting on channel
```

Finder is running, but `list_windows` for Finder returns no window. Finder never gets the request.

### Environment

Cua component and version/commit: cua-driver 0.34.0 and 0.41.3 (release binaries). The same code is on `main` at 0352bdc1ca364348469d882870418cbf39fd2bf7.
Operating system and architecture: macOS 26.7.2, arm64 (reported); probes below run on macOS 27.2, arm64.
Application/browser/window system/image: Finder, embedded host that starts `serve --no-overlay`.

### Evidence

Code path at 0352bdc1ca364348469d882870418cbf39fd2bf7:

- `open_finder_folders` sends `selectFile(nil, inFileViewerRootedAtPath:)` to the main queue with `dispatch_async_f` and waits up to 5 s ([apps/mod.rs#L505-L526](https://github.com/trycua/cua/blob/0352bdc1ca364348469d882870418cbf39fd2bf7/libs/cua-driver/rust/crates/platform-macos/src/apps/mod.rs#L505-L526)). #2754 added this, and says the driver "dispatches the narrow Finder handoff to its existing AppKit main queue".
- In `serve`, only the overlay path runs a main loop. With `--no-overlay`, the main thread only calls `serve_handle.join()` ([cua-driver/src/main.rs#L776-L787](https://github.com/trycua/cua/blob/0352bdc1ca364348469d882870418cbf39fd2bf7/libs/cua-driver/rust/crates/cua-driver/src/main.rs#L776-L787)). Before #7481, `--experimental-pip` gave a second main loop; now there is none.

Standalone probes (C / Objective-C, macOS 27.2 arm64). A worker thread sends one block to the main queue with `dispatch_async_f` and waits:

| Main thread does | Result |
| --- | --- |
| `pthread_join` (same as `serve_handle.join()`) | timeout: block never runs |
| `dispatch_main()` | block runs, but `pthread_main_np() == 0` and `[NSThread isMainThread] == NO`, so AppKit main-thread work is not safe there |
| `CFRunLoopRun()`, no `NSApplication` | block runs on the real main thread (`pthread_main_np() == 1`) |

More results with `CFRunLoopRun()` on the main thread and no `NSApplication`:

- It stayed blocked while idle. Three blocks sent at 0 s, 3 s and 8 s all ran on the main thread, with about 5 ms CPU in total over 9 s. It never returned.
- `[[NSWorkspace sharedWorkspace] selectFile:nil inFileViewerRootedAtPath:<temp folder>]`, sent from a worker thread to the main queue, returned `YES`, and Finder opened a window for the folder.

### Workaround and additional context

Workaround for agents: open the folder with the `open` command, or open Finder with no URL and go to the folder.

Possible fix (not built locally: `apple-metal` / `apple-cf` SwiftPM manifests do not link with the Command Line Tools on this machine). When the cursor overlay is off, keep the main thread serving the main queue and move the join to a watcher thread, so the process still exits when the serve thread ends:

```rust
// platform-macos/src/cursor/overlay.rs
pub fn run_main_queue() -> ! {
    #[link(name = "CoreFoundation", kind = "framework")]
    extern "C" {
        fn CFRunLoopRun();
    }
    loop {
        // SAFETY: called on the main thread; CFRunLoopRun has no preconditions.
        unsafe { CFRunLoopRun() };
    }
}

// cua-driver/src/main.rs, `serve`
if cursor_cfg.enabled {
    platform_macos::cursor::overlay::run_on_main_thread();
    let _ = serve_handle.join();
} else {
    std::thread::Builder::new()
        .name("cua-serve-join".into())
        .spawn(move || {
            let _ = serve_handle.join();
            std::process::exit(0);
        })
        .expect("spawn serve join thread");
    platform_macos::cursor::overlay::run_main_queue();
}
```

`CFRunLoopRun()` creates no `NSApplication`, so the daemon gets no Dock icon and no activation. It also does not touch the Window Server, so a headless start is not affected. Not checked: the same park in `run_on_main_thread()` when there is no graphic session (#1724 path); there the Finder open cannot work anyway.

Hosts that default to `--no-overlay` on macOS (for example, Hermes Agent's `computer_use.no_overlay` default) probably see the same failure.

